WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.387
Welcome to Day 4 of Case Management Training with Legal Support Help.

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:08.474
We'll keep using our sample case, John Doe versus Robert Smith,

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:11.320
the same file you're working on in the platform.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.818
Today we take the other road from the demand.

00:00:14.168 --> 00:00:17.740
Keystone held at three thousand five hundred dollars,

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.111
and our Time-Limited Demand lapsed on August 21st,

00:00:21.111 --> 00:00:21.448
2026.

00:00:21.798 --> 00:00:25.780
Because Keystone's ten thousand dollar Bodily Injury limit was never paid,

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.439
the whole forty thousand dollar recovery stack sat frozen behind it,

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:31.376
and Attorney Thorne authorized suit.

00:00:31.726 --> 00:00:34.651
So what happens when two sides still can't agree?

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:38.693
First comes mediation, where everyone works toward a compromise.

00:00:39.043 --> 00:00:43.571
Then, if that isn't enough, arbitration, where someone else makes the call.

00:00:43.921 --> 00:00:48.017
You'll see how your scheduling, your binders and your eye for detail decide how

00:00:48.017 --> 00:00:49.935
ready the attorney is when it counts.

00:00:50.285 --> 00:00:51.365
Let's dive in.

00:00:53.915 --> 00:00:58.118
Today has three parts, and they follow the order our file moves in.

00:00:58.468 --> 00:01:03.128
First, the Mediation phase, where the goal is a deal both sides can live with,

00:01:03.128 --> 00:01:07.071
and where your job is mostly scheduling, logistics and the binder.

00:01:07.421 --> 00:01:12.796
On our file, that's the court-ordered mediation on March 18th, 2027.

00:01:13.146 --> 00:01:17.999
Second, the Arbitration phase, where the case is decided for the parties instead.

00:01:18.349 --> 00:01:22.273
For John Doe, that's the binding UM arbitration that follows.

00:01:22.623 --> 00:01:25.607
Here the stakes rise, because the decision is final.

00:01:25.957 --> 00:01:30.506
And third, the common bottlenecks in both, the places where cases like John's tend

00:01:30.506 --> 00:01:34.223
to stall, so you can spot trouble early and keep your files moving.

00:01:36.773 --> 00:01:40.941
Before we get into either phase, let's meet the person in the middle, the neutral.

00:01:41.291 --> 00:01:45.906
In both mediation and arbitration, there is someone who belongs to neither side,

00:01:45.906 --> 00:01:48.617
but the two roles call for different strengths.

00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:53.594
A mediator is usually a seasoned personal injury trial attorney or a retired judge,

00:01:53.594 --> 00:01:56.270
and their gift is moving people toward a number.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:00.250
They understand negotiation, they know how liens can block a deal,

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.771
and they can tell both sides what a case is realistically worth.

00:02:04.121 --> 00:02:08.696
On our file, the mediator is the Honorable Carla Benton, a retired judge.

00:02:09.046 --> 00:02:13.298
An arbitrator is usually a retired judge or a senior trial attorney,

00:02:13.298 --> 00:02:16.800
and their gift is the law itself: the rules of evidence,

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.052
tort law, liability standards and medical causation.

00:02:20.402 --> 00:02:23.037
Our arbitrator is Laura Whitcomb, an attorney.

00:02:23.387 --> 00:02:24.630
Why should you care?

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:29.201
Because knowing who is in the room tells you what the attorney needs from you:

00:02:29.201 --> 00:02:32.503
persuasion material for one, airtight evidence for the other.

00:02:35.053 --> 00:02:37.363
So how does a neutral end up on a case?

00:02:37.713 --> 00:02:39.965
For a mediator, there are two paths.

00:02:40.315 --> 00:02:44.909
Sometimes the parties agree on someone known for fair evaluations after reviewing

00:02:44.909 --> 00:02:45.702
panel resumes.

00:02:46.052 --> 00:02:50.577
Other times a judge orders mediation, and the mediator comes from a court-approved

00:02:50.577 --> 00:02:50.963
roster.

00:02:51.313 --> 00:02:56.559
On our file, the court's Scheduling Order required mediation to be completed by

00:02:56.559 --> 00:03:00.543
March 31st, 2027, and Attorney Thorne selected Judge Benton.

00:03:00.893 --> 00:03:02.658
Arbitrators arrive differently.

00:03:03.008 --> 00:03:07.282
Often arbitration isn't a choice at all, because a policy contract or a

00:03:07.282 --> 00:03:09.510
post-mediation agreement requires it.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.903
That's exactly our situation.

00:03:12.253 --> 00:03:16.390
After mediation, Harbor Point and Cardinal State held firm on damages,

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:19.463
so Harbor Point's UM arbitration clause was invoked,

00:03:19.463 --> 00:03:22.714
and Cardinal State joined one consolidated arbitration.

00:03:23.063 --> 00:03:27.916
The slide also describes the panel strike list used by bodies like AAA and JAMS.

00:03:28.266 --> 00:03:32.379
That's general information worth knowing: both sides vet the candidates,

00:03:32.379 --> 00:03:35.407
rank them and strike names until one neutral is left.

00:03:35.757 --> 00:03:39.251
Our file runs on the carriers' arbitration clause instead.

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:44.123
Here is the most important difference of all.

00:03:44.473 --> 00:03:47.259
Think of the mediator as a facilitator, not a judge.

00:03:47.609 --> 00:03:51.069
A mediator can't force a settlement, can't rule on anything,

00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:52.799
and can't order anyone to pay.

00:03:53.149 --> 00:03:57.050
Our client keeps 100% control over whether to sign.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.138
On our file, Judge Benton didn't make Keystone do anything.

00:04:01.488 --> 00:04:05.250
Keystone chose to tender its ten thousand dollar limits at the mediation.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:07.562
An arbitrator is the opposite.

00:04:07.912 --> 00:04:10.907
Think of a private judge who also decides the facts.

00:04:11.257 --> 00:04:15.130
The arbitrator issues a binding award, decides liability,

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.119
sets damages, and can even exclude evidence.

00:04:18.469 --> 00:04:22.660
And once that award comes down, appeal rights are virtually non-existent.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:28.266
On July 6th, 2027, Laura Whitcomb awarded twenty thousand dollars against Harbor

00:04:28.266 --> 00:04:31.288
Point and ten thousand against Cardinal State.

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:32.903
That decision was final.

00:04:33.253 --> 00:04:37.282
Let that sink in, because it explains everything that follows today.

00:04:39.832 --> 00:04:44.522
Let's start with mediation, and with the part that falls squarely on you: logistics.

00:04:44.872 --> 00:04:49.342
Your job is to set the stage for compromise and keep the negotiations confidential.

00:04:49.692 --> 00:04:52.351
First, getting everyone on the same calendar.

00:04:52.701 --> 00:04:55.477
Gather three to four workable windows from the client,

00:04:55.477 --> 00:04:59.795
the handling attorney and opposing counsel before you ever reach out to the neutral.

00:05:00.145 --> 00:05:04.250
On our file, four windows were collected from Attorney Thorne,

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.693
John Doe and defense counsel on December 17th, 2026,

00:05:07.693 --> 00:05:09.746
before Judge Benton was chosen.

00:05:10.096 --> 00:05:13.884
That's why the Notice of Mediation could set a firm date:

00:05:13.884 --> 00:05:17.074
March 18th, 2027, at nine thirty in the morning.

00:05:17.424 --> 00:05:19.432
Second, controlling the environment.

00:05:19.782 --> 00:05:23.951
Mediation involves private strategy talks, so the setting has to protect them,

00:05:23.951 --> 00:05:28.386
whether that's a secure room with somewhere for the client to wait or a stable Zoom

00:05:28.386 --> 00:05:30.150
link with private breakout rooms.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.795
Our session is at Judge Benton's office, and John's attendance is required.

00:05:35.145 --> 00:05:38.524
So plan for where he'll be while the attorney talks strategy.

00:05:41.074 --> 00:05:43.751
Now the heart of your mediation prep: the binder,

00:05:43.751 --> 00:05:45.718
sometimes called a mediation packet.

00:05:46.068 --> 00:05:50.723
It's the most critical documents in the case, gathered and organized in one place.

00:05:51.073 --> 00:05:54.789
The best way to think of it is as a cheat sheet for the attorney and the mediator.

00:05:55.139 --> 00:05:56.927
Why does that matter so much?

00:05:57.277 --> 00:05:58.646
Picture the session.

00:05:58.996 --> 00:06:03.269
Defense counsel raises a point, and our attorney has seconds to answer it.

00:06:03.619 --> 00:06:07.775
There's no time to flip through loose paper or hunt through folders on a laptop.

00:06:08.125 --> 00:06:11.039
The attorney needs that medical record, that clause,

00:06:11.039 --> 00:06:14.569
or that line of deposition testimony within about five seconds.

00:06:14.919 --> 00:06:17.171
On our file, there's a hard date attached.

00:06:17.521 --> 00:06:22.671
The Confidential Mediation Statement and the binder are due to Judge Benton seven

00:06:22.671 --> 00:06:25.532
days before the session, on March 11th, 2027.

00:06:25.882 --> 00:06:28.750
So the binder isn't something you finish the night before.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.980
It goes out a full week ahead.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:36.793
Another way to see the binder is as the roadmap to settlement.

00:06:37.143 --> 00:06:41.674
It pulls together the key medical records, the special damages you've already

00:06:41.674 --> 00:06:45.676
verified, the liability reports, and the exhibits the case turns on.

00:06:46.026 --> 00:06:49.358
On John Doe's file, verified really means verified.

00:06:49.708 --> 00:06:54.033
The audited specials total sixteen thousand four hundred one dollars and forty

00:06:54.033 --> 00:06:58.357
cents, after a duplicate visit line from Riverton Spine and Rehab was removed.

00:06:58.707 --> 00:07:03.793
The liability report is the police report citing Robert Smith for failing to stop at

00:07:03.793 --> 00:07:04.640
the stop sign.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:07.660
But collecting documents is only half the job.

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:10.530
The other half is making them easy to find.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:14.281
That's why careful indexing and bookmarking matter so much.

00:07:14.631 --> 00:07:18.952
When the conversation moves fast, the attorney and the mediator should land on the

00:07:18.952 --> 00:07:20.111
right page in seconds.

00:07:20.461 --> 00:07:22.505
So how should the binder be organized?

00:07:22.855 --> 00:07:25.153
Let's walk through it, section by section.

00:07:27.703 --> 00:07:30.739
A strong mediation binder usually has six sections,

00:07:30.739 --> 00:07:34.785
and section one is the executive summary and administrative details.

00:07:35.135 --> 00:07:39.350
This is what the attorney sees first, so it sets the frame for everything else.

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:41.116
It holds three things.

00:07:41.466 --> 00:07:44.525
The CM snapshot, a single page showing the parties,

00:07:44.525 --> 00:07:47.585
our file numbers and how to reach opposing counsel.

00:07:47.935 --> 00:07:52.430
For us, that's John Doe versus Robert Smith in the State Court of Riverton County,

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:55.829
with defense counsel Attorney Grant Mercer of Mercer and Hale.

00:07:56.179 --> 00:08:01.008
Next, the mediation order or agreement, which sets the rules and how the mediator's

00:08:01.008 --> 00:08:01.764
fee is split.

00:08:02.114 --> 00:08:04.343
Read the split from the document itself.

00:08:04.693 --> 00:08:05.680
Never assume it.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:10.902
And then the schedule: March 18th, 2027, nine thirty in the morning,

00:08:10.902 --> 00:08:12.694
at Judge Benton's office.

00:08:13.044 --> 00:08:15.855
Think of this section as answering the basic who,

00:08:15.855 --> 00:08:19.870
when, where, and on what terms before the attorney turns another page.

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:26.925
Section two holds the briefs, meaning each side's legal and factual arguments.

00:08:27.275 --> 00:08:28.575
You'll include two.

00:08:28.925 --> 00:08:30.504
Ours is confidential.

00:08:30.854 --> 00:08:34.218
The handling attorney writes it to lay out our strongest points,

00:08:34.218 --> 00:08:36.845
our key evidence and where we stand on settlement.

00:08:37.195 --> 00:08:42.862
On our file, that's the Confidential Mediation Statement Attorney Thorne sends Judge

00:08:42.862 --> 00:08:44.683
Benton by March 11th, 2027.

00:08:45.033 --> 00:08:47.193
Then there's the brief from opposing counsel.

00:08:47.543 --> 00:08:49.714
Why include the other side's brief?

00:08:50.064 --> 00:08:54.381
Because it lets our attorney read both arguments side by side and get rebuttals

00:08:54.381 --> 00:08:55.474
ready ahead of time.

00:08:55.824 --> 00:08:58.897
On this file, you can already predict the defense themes,

00:08:58.897 --> 00:09:02.077
because they've been the same since Keystone's first offer:

00:09:02.077 --> 00:09:05.635
the fourteen-day treatment gap, and an MRI they call degenerative.

00:09:05.985 --> 00:09:09.758
When the defense raises them in the room, our attorney has seen them coming.

00:09:10.108 --> 00:09:14.067
Your part is making sure both briefs are there, current, and easy to reach.

00:09:16.617 --> 00:09:20.135
Section three is the core pleadings, the legal frame of the case.

00:09:20.485 --> 00:09:24.606
These are the formal court filings that define what the lawsuit is actually about.

00:09:24.956 --> 00:09:29.716
First, the active complaint, the operative version of what our client alleges.

00:09:30.066 --> 00:09:35.613
Ours was e-filed on September 24th, 2026, and it pleads the medical specials,

00:09:35.613 --> 00:09:39.575
lost wages, future medical care and pain and suffering.

00:09:39.925 --> 00:09:44.293
Then the answer and any affirmative defenses, the other side's formal response and

00:09:44.293 --> 00:09:46.264
the legal shields they're relying on.

00:09:46.614 --> 00:09:50.541
Attorney Mercer's answer, filed November 12th, 2026,

00:09:50.541 --> 00:09:53.638
denies the claim and pleads three things.

00:09:53.988 --> 00:09:58.354
Comparative negligence: that the Escape entered the all-way stop out of turn,

00:09:58.354 --> 00:09:59.886
and that John was unbelted.

00:10:00.236 --> 00:10:03.579
Causation: the gap and the so-called degenerative MRI.

00:10:03.929 --> 00:10:06.704
And failure to mitigate: the disrupted care.

00:10:07.054 --> 00:10:11.736
Having the two side by side keeps everyone anchored to what's really in dispute,

00:10:11.736 --> 00:10:15.599
and tells our attorney exactly which defenses we need to overcome.

00:10:18.149 --> 00:10:20.506
Section four is where the case gets proven.

00:10:20.856 --> 00:10:25.384
The slide calls these the smoking guns, the raw evidence that shows we're right.

00:10:25.734 --> 00:10:29.019
The contract and email examples are for business disputes.

00:10:29.369 --> 00:10:33.897
In a personal injury case like ours, it's the official report of what happened.

00:10:34.247 --> 00:10:36.994
For John Doe, that's the Riverton police report,

00:10:36.994 --> 00:10:40.656
where Robert Smith was cited for failure to stop at a stop sign.

00:10:41.006 --> 00:10:44.460
Then the photographs and video stills, showing the damage,

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.068
the scene and the injuries.

00:10:46.418 --> 00:10:50.811
Our file has something even better: doorbell-camera video from Margaret Doe's house

00:10:50.811 --> 00:10:53.616
showing the Escape stopped and Smith rolling through.

00:10:53.966 --> 00:10:58.575
Finally, the deposition summaries, with the exact transcript pages highlighted.

00:10:58.925 --> 00:11:04.608
John was deposed on February 18th, 2027, and defense counsel worked the gap,

00:11:04.608 --> 00:11:08.944
the disruption, his 2021 low-back visits and the seatbelt.

00:11:09.294 --> 00:11:11.187
Why both a summary and the pages?

00:11:11.537 --> 00:11:15.124
So the attorney can scan fast and still point to the precise words.

00:11:17.674 --> 00:11:22.121
If section four proves what happened, section five proves what it cost.

00:11:22.471 --> 00:11:25.605
This is where we justify the dollar figure we're asking for.

00:11:25.955 --> 00:11:30.644
Start with the medical side: the records summary with chart notes in date order,

00:11:30.644 --> 00:11:33.281
and a ledger of every medical expense so far.

00:11:33.631 --> 00:11:37.689
On our file, the medical specials come to fourteen thousand eight hundred seventeen

00:11:37.689 --> 00:11:39.645
dollars and forty cents after the audit.

00:11:39.995 --> 00:11:42.073
Then the proof of financial loss.

00:11:42.423 --> 00:11:47.011
For John, that's nine missed shifts, one thousand five hundred eighty-four dollars,

00:11:47.011 --> 00:11:49.830
verified by Northgate's wage-and-attendance letter.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.257
Last come the supporting medical and expert opinions.

00:11:53.607 --> 00:11:55.627
The slide's examples are general.

00:11:55.977 --> 00:11:59.359
On our file, the key one is Doctor Reyes's Causation Letter,

00:11:59.359 --> 00:12:03.361
which says the injury was more likely than not caused by the collision.

00:12:03.711 --> 00:12:06.347
As a case manager, your eye is on accuracy.

00:12:06.697 --> 00:12:10.375
A ledger that doesn't match the records weakens the very number this section is

00:12:10.375 --> 00:12:11.166
meant to support.

00:12:13.716 --> 00:12:17.257
Section six closes the binder, and it's about getting the deal done.

00:12:17.607 --> 00:12:22.095
First, the negotiation log, every demand and every counter-offer in order,

00:12:22.095 --> 00:12:24.945
so no one argues about where the numbers stand.

00:12:25.295 --> 00:12:30.178
On our file, that's the July 20th, 2026 demand for Keystone's ten thousand dollar

00:12:30.178 --> 00:12:34.217
limits, the three thousand five hundred dollar offer on August 5th,

00:12:34.217 --> 00:12:36.568
and Keystone calling that number final.

00:12:36.918 --> 00:12:39.856
Second, a draft settlement agreement or release,

00:12:39.856 --> 00:12:42.549
ready to sign before anyone leaves the room.

00:12:42.899 --> 00:12:47.823
Here the draft has to be a Limited Release, covering Robert Smith and Keystone only,

00:12:47.823 --> 00:12:52.688
with the UM carriers' consent, so the thirty thousand dollars of UM coverage behind

00:12:52.688 --> 00:12:53.743
Keystone survives.

00:12:54.093 --> 00:12:58.581
That's exactly what happened at our mediation: Keystone tendered its limits,

00:12:58.581 --> 00:13:02.420
the UM consents were obtained, the release was a Limited Release,

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:04.251
and Robert Smith was dismissed.

00:13:04.601 --> 00:13:05.913
And a practical tip.

00:13:06.263 --> 00:13:09.982
For in-person mediation, make three binders: for the attorney,

00:13:09.982 --> 00:13:11.662
the mediator and the client.

00:13:12.012 --> 00:13:16.586
For virtual mediation, run OCR on the PDF so it's keyword-searchable.

00:13:19.136 --> 00:13:22.770
Once the binder is built, you shift into a quality assurance role,

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:25.963
the final check that the attorney walks in fully prepared.

00:13:26.313 --> 00:13:27.822
Three things to confirm.

00:13:28.172 --> 00:13:33.051
First, multiple sets: three identical physical copies for the handling attorney,

00:13:33.051 --> 00:13:37.565
the mediator and the client, or one shared digital setup they can all use.

00:13:37.914 --> 00:13:42.512
Our session is in person at Judge Benton's office, so think three physical copies.

00:13:42.862 --> 00:13:44.360
Second, searchability.

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:50.200
If any version is digital, run a full OCR text-recognition scan so evidence can be

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:51.606
found with Control F.

00:13:51.956 --> 00:13:56.809
Third, indexing: six numbered divider tabs that match the firm's master index.

00:13:57.159 --> 00:14:00.178
See how that lines up with the six sections we just covered?

00:14:00.528 --> 00:14:02.037
And remember the calendar.

00:14:02.387 --> 00:14:07.504
On our file, all of this has to be finished before the binder goes to Judge Benton

00:14:07.504 --> 00:14:10.874
on March 11th, 2027, not on the morning of March 18th.

00:14:13.424 --> 00:14:15.630
Now let's move into arbitration.

00:14:15.979 --> 00:14:19.362
In PI practice, when a motor vehicle accident, or MVA,

00:14:19.362 --> 00:14:23.309
case or a commercial trucking case doesn't settle at mediation,

00:14:23.309 --> 00:14:24.687
it often ends up here.

00:14:25.037 --> 00:14:27.498
Our file took a slightly different path.

00:14:27.848 --> 00:14:32.341
Mediation did resolve the Bodily Injury piece when Keystone tendered its limits.

00:14:32.691 --> 00:14:37.124
Then the UM carriers made low offers, eight thousand from Harbor Point and six

00:14:37.124 --> 00:14:40.249
thousand from Cardinal State, and held firm on damages.

00:14:40.599 --> 00:14:45.465
So Harbor Point's UM arbitration clause sent the rest to one consolidated binding

00:14:45.465 --> 00:14:47.507
arbitration before Laura Whitcomb.

00:14:47.857 --> 00:14:49.459
What's your role at the hearing?

00:14:49.809 --> 00:14:52.561
Think of yourself as the attorney's eyes and memory.

00:14:52.911 --> 00:14:57.606
Observe and document the witness testimony, opposing counsel's focus and how the

00:14:57.606 --> 00:14:59.366
arbitrator reacts to evidence.

00:14:59.716 --> 00:15:04.428
Log directives, recording any post-hearing brief timelines or rulings from the bench

00:15:04.428 --> 00:15:05.045
right away.

00:15:05.395 --> 00:15:08.685
And update the CMS back at the office with the new deadlines,

00:15:08.685 --> 00:15:11.490
along with a summary memo for the handling attorney.

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:16.548
Arbitration calls for a different mindset.

00:15:16.898 --> 00:15:19.278
In mediation we focused on compromise.

00:15:19.628 --> 00:15:22.113
Here, we prepare as if we're going to trial.

00:15:22.462 --> 00:15:23.519
Why the change?

00:15:23.869 --> 00:15:26.435
Mediation is voluntary and non-binding.

00:15:26.785 --> 00:15:29.536
It's a negotiation, and nobody has to agree.

00:15:29.886 --> 00:15:32.452
Arbitration is adversarial and formal.

00:15:32.802 --> 00:15:37.254
A neutral third party, the arbitrator, sits as the judge and issues a final,

00:15:37.254 --> 00:15:39.246
binding decision called the award.

00:15:39.596 --> 00:15:43.346
Once the award comes down, the law sharply limits any appeal.

00:15:43.696 --> 00:15:45.867
Think about what that meant for John Doe.

00:15:46.217 --> 00:15:50.661
Everything still open on his file, the thirty thousand dollars of UM coverage from

00:15:50.661 --> 00:15:55.052
Harbor Point and Cardinal State, was riding on one hearing before Laura Whitcomb.

00:15:55.402 --> 00:15:59.163
There was no second chance to fix a missing exhibit or a late brief.

00:15:59.513 --> 00:16:03.831
That's why you treat an arbitration hearing with the same precision as a multi-day

00:16:03.831 --> 00:16:05.516
trial in state or federal court.

00:16:05.866 --> 00:16:08.652
Everything that follows comes from that mindset.

00:16:11.202 --> 00:16:14.577
On the arbitration track, most of your work is operational:

00:16:14.577 --> 00:16:19.039
tracking timelines, keeping strict schedules and managing documents carefully.

00:16:19.389 --> 00:16:20.968
Let's start with the calendar.

00:16:21.318 --> 00:16:25.580
The moment a case goes to arbitration, the key dates become hard rules on the firm's

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:26.391
master calendar.

00:16:26.741 --> 00:16:30.731
First, the hearing date, the trial-like day when the attorney argues,

00:16:30.731 --> 00:16:32.813
cross-examines and puts in evidence.

00:16:33.163 --> 00:16:37.262
Ours is June 22nd, 2027, at nine in the morning.

00:16:37.612 --> 00:16:41.868
Second, the brief deadline, the absolute cutoff for our claims,

00:16:41.868 --> 00:16:44.705
arguments, exhibit index and witness list.

00:16:45.055 --> 00:16:47.609
Ours is due ten days before the hearing.

00:16:47.959 --> 00:16:53.683
That lands on Saturday, June 12th, so it's docketed for Friday, June 11th, 2027.

00:16:54.033 --> 00:16:58.503
When you count backward, you move to the earlier business day, never the later one.

00:16:58.853 --> 00:17:04.797
Our file also has an exhibit exchange twenty days out, on June 2nd, 2027.

00:17:05.147 --> 00:17:10.225
Third, the arbitrator selection cutoff, for vetting and striking names from a panel

00:17:10.225 --> 00:17:12.183
through bodies like AAA or JAMS.

00:17:12.533 --> 00:17:15.406
Our arbitrator came through the carriers' clause,

00:17:15.406 --> 00:17:18.280
but on a panel case, that cutoff is just as hard.

00:17:20.830 --> 00:17:24.336
The second track is logistics, and here you're the main coordinator.

00:17:24.686 --> 00:17:26.938
Notice how much this echoes mediation.

00:17:27.288 --> 00:17:31.562
You start by coordinating availability, working with the other side's staff to find

00:17:31.562 --> 00:17:34.085
three to four time blocks that work for everyone,

00:17:34.085 --> 00:17:36.402
before any formal outreach to the arbitrator.

00:17:36.752 --> 00:17:41.526
On our file, the hearing date came out of a scheduling call on April 23rd,

00:17:41.526 --> 00:17:45.912
2027, and Laura Whitcomb's Notice of Hearing followed on April 26th.

00:17:46.262 --> 00:17:50.971
Next, retain the neutral: vet the arbitrator, confirm the daily and hourly fees,

00:17:50.971 --> 00:17:54.680
and get the retainer deposits to accounting for prompt payment.

00:17:55.029 --> 00:17:57.061
Those fees are real case costs.

00:17:57.411 --> 00:18:00.622
Our share of Laura Whitcomb's fee came to nine hundred dollars,

00:18:00.622 --> 00:18:03.425
and it's one of the case costs in John's final numbers.

00:18:03.775 --> 00:18:08.090
Finally, handle logistics: calendar invites to everyone on our team,

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:12.912
and either a firm conference room or a secure virtual Zoom layout with clear

00:18:12.912 --> 00:18:13.864
breakout rooms.

00:18:14.214 --> 00:18:16.467
The difference from mediation is the stakes.

00:18:16.817 --> 00:18:19.417
Every detail now supports a binding hearing.

00:18:21.967 --> 00:18:23.465
Now an essential boundary.

00:18:23.815 --> 00:18:26.775
What can you do at a hearing, and what can't you do?

00:18:27.125 --> 00:18:28.460
Here's the golden rule.

00:18:28.810 --> 00:18:32.049
You may never present legal arguments, speak on the record,

00:18:32.049 --> 00:18:34.848
or represent the client in front of the arbitrator.

00:18:35.198 --> 00:18:39.087
Doing so is the unauthorized practice of law, or UPL.

00:18:39.437 --> 00:18:42.130
Think about our hearing before Laura Whitcomb.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:45.748
You may know John Doe's file better than anyone in the room,

00:18:45.748 --> 00:18:48.471
every visit, every date, every page of the binder.

00:18:48.821 --> 00:18:51.816
Even so, the advocacy belongs to Attorney Thorne.

00:18:52.166 --> 00:18:56.671
Your role is administrative help, technical support and careful observation.

00:18:57.021 --> 00:18:58.565
So what's left for you?

00:18:58.915 --> 00:18:59.890
Quite a lot.

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:04.498
You watch and take notes on the testimony, on what the other side keeps pushing,

00:19:04.498 --> 00:19:06.254
and on how the arbitrator reacts.

00:19:06.604 --> 00:19:10.366
You record any rulings or post-hearing deadlines the moment they're given.

00:19:10.716 --> 00:19:15.360
And back at the office, you update the CMS and send the attorney a summary memo.

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:21.185
This rule matters enough that the firm makes it formal policy,

00:19:21.185 --> 00:19:23.668
so let's think about what it means in practice.

00:19:24.018 --> 00:19:26.561
The UPL policy draws a clear line.

00:19:26.911 --> 00:19:31.335
Case managers don't argue the law, don't speak on the record and don't represent

00:19:31.335 --> 00:19:32.995
clients before the arbitrator.

00:19:33.345 --> 00:19:35.283
Where might you be tempted to cross it?

00:19:35.633 --> 00:19:39.768
Maybe the other side raises the 2021 low-back visits or the seatbelt,

00:19:39.768 --> 00:19:43.064
and you know exactly where the answer is in the binder.

00:19:43.414 --> 00:19:46.525
Or someone turns and asks you a question directly.

00:19:46.875 --> 00:19:51.113
The policy says your role stays administrative, technical and observational.

00:19:51.463 --> 00:19:54.162
You can quietly hand the attorney the right page,

00:19:54.162 --> 00:19:56.641
but you can't step into the advocate's shoes.

00:19:56.991 --> 00:20:00.257
It's the same line we held during John's deposition prep,

00:20:00.257 --> 00:20:04.038
where the case manager runs logistics and never coaches testimony.

00:20:04.388 --> 00:20:07.087
Within that boundary, your value is still huge:

00:20:07.087 --> 00:20:10.589
documenting the hearing, capturing directives from the bench,

00:20:10.589 --> 00:20:14.895
and getting the CMS and the attorney's memo updated as soon as you're back.

00:20:17.445 --> 00:20:20.696
Now let's revisit the binder, this time for arbitration.

00:20:21.046 --> 00:20:24.343
In mediation, the binder helped people reach a compromise.

00:20:24.693 --> 00:20:28.890
Here, it's a formal evidentiary record that goes straight to a private judge,

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:30.417
so it has to be trial-ready.

00:20:30.767 --> 00:20:32.427
Consider what can go wrong.

00:20:32.777 --> 00:20:37.335
Leave out a page, or print a key vehicle intrusion photo in faded grayscale,

00:20:37.335 --> 00:20:41.473
and the arbitrator can exclude it for good, with no chance of appeal.

00:20:41.823 --> 00:20:46.296
On our file, that intrusion is personal: Smith's truck struck the Escape's front

00:20:46.296 --> 00:20:48.812
passenger door, right where John was sitting.

00:20:49.162 --> 00:20:52.761
Like the mediation binder, this one has six tabbed sections.

00:20:53.111 --> 00:20:57.833
Section one is the arbitration submissions, the paperwork that sets the ground

00:20:57.833 --> 00:20:58.196
rules.

00:20:58.546 --> 00:21:00.543
Your case manager snapshot sheet.

00:21:00.893 --> 00:21:05.595
The final arbitration brief, docketed for June 11th, 2027.

00:21:05.945 --> 00:21:07.443
The governing order or agreement.

00:21:07.793 --> 00:21:12.775
For us, that's Harbor Point's UM arbitration clause and Laura Whitcomb's Notice of

00:21:12.775 --> 00:21:13.261
Hearing.

00:21:13.611 --> 00:21:16.705
And the arbitrator fee disclosures: rates, the split,

00:21:16.705 --> 00:21:19.857
and confirmation that retainer deposits were received.

00:21:22.407 --> 00:21:26.014
Section two gives the arbitrator the legal framework of the lawsuit,

00:21:26.014 --> 00:21:28.560
just like the pleadings in the mediation binder.

00:21:28.910 --> 00:21:32.898
First, the operative amended complaint, the version actually in force,

00:21:32.898 --> 00:21:35.575
setting out the specific counts we're pursuing.

00:21:35.925 --> 00:21:39.140
Here's a practical point: take the older complaints out.

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:44.116
If superseded versions sit in the binder, they can blur which claims are actually

00:21:44.116 --> 00:21:44.401
live.

00:21:44.751 --> 00:21:49.714
Second, the answer and affirmative defenses, showing how the defense plans to avoid

00:21:49.714 --> 00:21:51.926
liability or argue comparative fault.

00:21:52.276 --> 00:21:55.504
On John Doe's file, you already know that argument.

00:21:55.854 --> 00:22:00.117
The answer says the Escape entered the all-way stop out of turn and that John was

00:22:00.117 --> 00:22:00.591
unbelted.

00:22:00.941 --> 00:22:05.297
Putting those defenses right in front of the arbitrator tells our attorney exactly

00:22:05.297 --> 00:22:07.953
what the evidence in the next section has to beat.

00:22:10.503 --> 00:22:12.140
Section three proves fault.

00:22:12.490 --> 00:22:17.041
Its job is to show the adverse driver is 100% responsible for the impact.

00:22:17.391 --> 00:22:20.569
Start with the official police and incident reports,

00:22:20.569 --> 00:22:24.787
unredacted, with the officer's narrative, diagrams and any citations.

00:22:25.137 --> 00:22:29.766
On our file, the Riverton police report cites Robert Smith for failure to stop at a

00:22:29.766 --> 00:22:32.721
stop sign, and an independent witness, Ruth Calloway,

00:22:32.721 --> 00:22:34.785
gave a signed statement at the scene.

00:22:35.135 --> 00:22:38.396
Then the photos of the vehicle damage and the scene layout,

00:22:38.396 --> 00:22:39.779
in high-resolution color.

00:22:40.129 --> 00:22:41.522
Grayscale isn't allowed.

00:22:41.872 --> 00:22:43.172
Why so strict?

00:22:43.522 --> 00:22:48.079
Smith hit the Escape's front passenger door at about twenty-eight miles per hour and

00:22:48.079 --> 00:22:49.815
pushed it into the parked Civic.

00:22:50.165 --> 00:22:53.067
A washed-out black-and-white print can't show that,

00:22:53.067 --> 00:22:56.539
and that impact is exactly what this section exists to prove.

00:22:56.889 --> 00:22:59.745
This tab also answers the seatbelt defense.

00:23:00.095 --> 00:23:04.390
The Escape's event data recorder shows John's front-passenger belt was buckled.

00:23:06.940 --> 00:23:09.274
Section four turns from fault to injury.

00:23:09.624 --> 00:23:13.455
It justifies the compensation we're asking for John's physical injuries.

00:23:13.805 --> 00:23:15.047
Think of it in layers.

00:23:15.397 --> 00:23:19.609
On top is the consolidated medical ledger, the treatment chart in date order with a

00:23:19.609 --> 00:23:21.690
billing spreadsheet tracking total costs.

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:26.385
For us, that's fourteen thousand eight hundred seventeen dollars and forty cents in

00:23:26.385 --> 00:23:27.694
audited medical specials.

00:23:28.044 --> 00:23:31.303
Underneath are the provider treatment logs, the records,

00:23:31.303 --> 00:23:34.854
intake packets, chart notes and invoices from every facility,

00:23:34.854 --> 00:23:38.288
from Riverton Urgent Care through Riverton Spine and Rehab,

00:23:38.288 --> 00:23:41.140
Lakeside Orthopedics and Crescent Pain and Spine.

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:43.301
Then the diagnostic imaging.

00:23:43.651 --> 00:23:48.817
John's cervical MRI at Summit Imaging confirmed a C5-C6 disc herniation.

00:23:49.167 --> 00:23:53.790
Last, the slide lists Emergency Medical Condition declarations for medical

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:54.415
necessity.

00:23:54.765 --> 00:23:55.949
Be careful here.

00:23:56.299 --> 00:24:01.001
That's a Florida PIP rule, and our Riverton file has no EMC rule.

00:24:01.351 --> 00:24:03.859
You'd include it when you work a Florida file.

00:24:04.209 --> 00:24:07.805
Together, these layers answer the arbitrator's questions:

00:24:07.805 --> 00:24:11.338
what was done, what it cost, and what proves the injury.

00:24:13.888 --> 00:24:17.359
Section five is defensive, and you build it on purpose.

00:24:17.709 --> 00:24:22.101
Defense attorneys like to take a client's old records and argue that the current

00:24:22.101 --> 00:24:25.012
injury is just a pre-existing degenerative condition.

00:24:25.362 --> 00:24:28.578
This section is your shield, ready before they make the argument.

00:24:28.928 --> 00:24:32.115
It holds the prior injury records and work history,

00:24:32.115 --> 00:24:34.303
documenting past injuries honestly.

00:24:34.653 --> 00:24:38.531
The slide's example, a 2018 strain, is a general one.

00:24:38.881 --> 00:24:41.446
Here's what our file actually shows.

00:24:41.796 --> 00:24:47.038
In John Doe versus Robert Smith, our client's only prior care was three chiropractic

00:24:47.038 --> 00:24:51.282
visits in 2021 at Birch Avenue Chiropractic, for low-back stiffness.

00:24:51.632 --> 00:24:54.674
It resolved, and he had no prior neck complaints.

00:24:55.024 --> 00:24:57.311
His injury in this case is in his neck.

00:24:57.661 --> 00:25:01.387
The section also holds the medical opinions that answer the defense.

00:25:01.737 --> 00:25:04.427
Against the word degenerative, the radiologist,

00:25:04.427 --> 00:25:09.178
Doctor Helen Park, found no significant degenerative change at the adjacent levels,

00:25:09.178 --> 00:25:13.127
and Doctor Reyes's Causation Letter ties the injury to the collision.

00:25:13.477 --> 00:25:16.008
The lesson: don't hide old injuries.

00:25:16.358 --> 00:25:19.051
Document them so they can't be used against the client.

00:25:21.601 --> 00:25:25.780
The final section is the money, and it directly shapes what the client actually

00:25:25.780 --> 00:25:26.361
takes home.

00:25:26.711 --> 00:25:31.716
Start with lost wages, verified through W-2s, tax returns or employment

00:25:31.716 --> 00:25:32.632
verification.

00:25:32.982 --> 00:25:37.929
For John, that's nine missed shifts, one thousand five hundred eighty-four dollars,

00:25:37.929 --> 00:25:39.240
verified by Northgate.

00:25:39.590 --> 00:25:43.659
Then the negotiation ledger, the timeline of every demand and counter-offer,

00:25:43.659 --> 00:25:47.781
including Harbor Point's eight thousand dollar offer and Cardinal State's six

00:25:47.781 --> 00:25:48.263
thousand.

00:25:48.613 --> 00:25:53.124
Then the audited lien payout sheets, with health insurance holds and subrogation

00:25:53.124 --> 00:25:54.139
claims reconciled.

00:25:54.489 --> 00:25:56.463
This is where your attention pays off.

00:25:56.813 --> 00:25:59.982
Check each provider lien against what health insurance paid.

00:26:00.332 --> 00:26:01.969
Our file shows why.

00:26:02.319 --> 00:26:06.250
Crescent Pain and Spine billed three thousand four hundred dollars for John's

00:26:06.250 --> 00:26:10.181
injection, but Peach State paid it at the contract rate of one thousand eight

00:26:10.181 --> 00:26:13.500
hundred sixty dollars, and Crescent can't bill John for the rest.

00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:18.198
Catching that kind of thing stops a provider from collecting the full face value out

00:26:18.198 --> 00:26:19.492
of the client's recovery.

00:26:22.042 --> 00:26:26.732
To wrap up the day, let's look at where cases get stuck, starting with mediation.

00:26:27.082 --> 00:26:29.161
Think of these as compromise traps.

00:26:29.511 --> 00:26:33.028
The first is a medical ledger or lien picture that isn't verified.

00:26:33.378 --> 00:26:38.647
Unreconciled hospital bills or missing final treatment records leave the defense

00:26:38.647 --> 00:26:40.623
with damages it can't confirm.

00:26:40.973 --> 00:26:45.476
On our file, Riverton Spine and Rehab's ledger still carries a duplicate visit,

00:26:45.476 --> 00:26:50.149
so the audited figure of six thousand one hundred five dollars has to stand on the

00:26:50.149 --> 00:26:51.004
attendance log.

00:26:51.354 --> 00:26:53.757
The second trap is missing decision-makers.

00:26:54.107 --> 00:26:58.397
If the adjuster or defense representative in the room lacks full settlement

00:26:58.397 --> 00:27:01.944
authority, and nothing was pre-negotiated, nobody can say yes.

00:27:02.294 --> 00:27:04.012
The third is brief misalignment.

00:27:04.362 --> 00:27:09.567
A brief that arrives late, or without key exhibits like MRIs and crash diagrams,

00:27:09.567 --> 00:27:11.584
leaves the mediator unprepared.

00:27:11.934 --> 00:27:16.090
That's why our statement and binder were due to Judge Benton a full week ahead.

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:20.178
Notice that the first and third traps are things your own prep can prevent.

00:27:22.728 --> 00:27:26.551
Arbitration has its own traps, and since we're preparing for trial,

00:27:26.551 --> 00:27:28.092
think of them as landmines.

00:27:28.442 --> 00:27:33.150
First, evidence gets excluded because a document is missing or a photo is a poor

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:34.444
black-and-white print.

00:27:34.794 --> 00:27:37.105
Remember the color rule from section three.

00:27:37.455 --> 00:27:41.739
On our file, that's the color photo of the Escape's struck passenger door.

00:27:42.089 --> 00:27:46.911
Second, pre-existing injury traps, where prior injury records like past strains

00:27:46.911 --> 00:27:49.659
aren't backed by expert medical declarations.

00:27:50.008 --> 00:27:55.465
For John, that's his 2021 low-back visits, which defense counsel already raised at

00:27:55.465 --> 00:27:56.464
his deposition.

00:27:56.814 --> 00:27:59.763
The prior medical shield exists for exactly this.

00:28:00.113 --> 00:28:04.938
Third, calendar and deadline misses: arbitrator scheduling windows that aren't

00:28:04.938 --> 00:28:08.031
coordinated, or a hard brief cutoff that slips by.

00:28:08.381 --> 00:28:11.996
Picture docketing our brief for Saturday, June 12th,

00:28:11.996 --> 00:28:14.429
instead of Friday, June 11th, 2027.

00:28:14.779 --> 00:28:18.297
Each of these landmines connects back to something we covered today.

00:28:20.847 --> 00:28:22.647
So how do you stay ahead of all this?

00:28:22.997 --> 00:28:24.738
Two habits make a big difference.

00:28:25.088 --> 00:28:27.654
First, the 48-hour pre-session audit.

00:28:28.004 --> 00:28:32.975
Build in a mandatory file review 48 hours before any deadline to check the ledgers

00:28:32.975 --> 00:28:35.098
and confirm the binder is complete.

00:28:35.447 --> 00:28:40.594
On our file, that means auditing before the mediation binder goes to Judge Benton on

00:28:40.594 --> 00:28:44.515
March 11th, 2027, and before the arbitration brief on June 11th.

00:28:44.865 --> 00:28:48.603
That buffer gives you time to fix a problem instead of discovering it in the room.

00:28:48.953 --> 00:28:51.020
Second, reconcile liens early.

00:28:51.370 --> 00:28:54.581
Review the health insurance Explanation of Benefits,

00:28:54.581 --> 00:28:59.521
the EOBs, well in advance, so you can catch double-dipping hospital and provider

00:28:59.521 --> 00:28:59.892
liens.

00:29:00.242 --> 00:29:03.885
Crescent's injection, paid by Peach State at the contract rate,

00:29:03.885 --> 00:29:06.662
is exactly the kind of line that check protects.

00:29:07.012 --> 00:29:11.667
One habit protects the evidence, and the other protects the client's recovery.

00:29:14.217 --> 00:29:16.377
That brings us to the end of Day 4.

00:29:16.727 --> 00:29:20.915
Today we met the neutrals and saw why a mediator and an arbitrator call for such

00:29:20.915 --> 00:29:22.067
different preparation.

00:29:22.417 --> 00:29:26.925
We built the mediation binder, and watched John Doe's mediation before Judge Benton

00:29:26.925 --> 00:29:30.347
end with Keystone tendering its limits under a Limited Release.

00:29:30.697 --> 00:29:34.006
Then we moved into a trial-ready mindset for arbitration,

00:29:34.006 --> 00:29:37.721
drew a clear line around what you can and can't do at a hearing,

00:29:37.721 --> 00:29:42.365
and built the six-section arbitration binder that carried John's UM claims to an

00:29:42.365 --> 00:29:45.790
award of twenty thousand dollars plus ten thousand dollars.

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:50.528
Finally, we looked at the traps that stall these cases and the habits that prevent

00:29:50.528 --> 00:29:50.795
them.

00:29:51.145 --> 00:29:54.646
Take some time to review the John Doe versus Robert Smith file,

00:29:54.646 --> 00:29:58.425
because the precision you bring here protects the client's recovery.

00:29:58.775 --> 00:29:59.390
Thank you.

